WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.160
The Star Wars film series is one of the most
commercially and critically successful franchises

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:05.880
in history.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:10.780
Ticket sales alone have yielded over $9.5
billion worldwide to date — and that's not

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:13.440
counting merch sales, licensing deals, and
other profits.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:18.480
Since its start in 1977, the series has grown
from a trilogy of well-received fantasy movies

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:23.660
into a full-blown, worldwide institution,
applauded for its ability to excite and inspire

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:24.340
audiences.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:28.080
Of course, this isn't to say that the Star
Wars franchise is completely perfect.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:28.840
Far from it.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.500
Fans can, and will, pick apart the movies
and find dozens of slip-ups and make a laundry

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:37.239
list of less-than-awesome moments that slightly
mar the overall pictures.

00:00:37.239 --> 00:00:41.770
But those little moments would be small potatoes
in comparison to the mistake Disney has admitted

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:46.100
to making with the Star Wars franchise — one
that the studio is trying to remedy as we

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:46.600
speak.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:51.379
During a September 2018 interview with The
Hollywood Reporter, Walt Disney Company CEO

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:57.200
Bob Iger addressed the fact that, after acquiring
Lucasfilm for just over $4 billion in 2012,

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:01.520
Disney has churned out one Star Wars movie
every year since 2015.

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:07.659
Specifically, those movies would be J.J. Abrams'
Episode 7: The Force Awakens, the series-first

00:01:07.659 --> 00:01:13.640
anthology film Rogue One in 2016, the Rian
Johnson-directed Episode 8: The Last Jedi

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:19.100
in December 2017, and the "young Han Solo"
movie, simply called Solo, just five months

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:20.760
later in May 2018.

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:25.040
When reporter Matthew Belloni brought up the
seemingly growing impression that Disney should

00:01:25.049 --> 00:01:30.219
pump the brakes on its Star Wars releases,
and not put out a new movie each year, Iger

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.380
surprisingly agreed.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.579
He actually admitted that the huge volume
of releases right out of the gate was a total

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:40.250
mistake, and even took personal responsibility
for the evident, fatigue-inducing misstep.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:41.240
Iger confessed,

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:45.660
"As I look back, I think the mistake that
I made — I take the blame — was a little

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:46.920
too much, too fast."

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:51.420
Looking ahead, the CEO said that Disney will
no longer be producing Star Wars movies at

00:01:51.429 --> 00:01:53.159
such an accelerated pace.

00:01:53.159 --> 00:01:58.170
According to Iger, fans "can expect some slowdown"
following the release of JJ Abrams' Episode

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:02.179
9 — although no one should expect the House
of Mouse to bring things to a total halt.

00:02:02.179 --> 00:02:07.079
As Iger explained, the state of Star Wars
movies beyond 2019's Episode 9 will still

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:09.620
be robust — just with a little more breathing
room.

00:02:09.849 --> 00:02:14.940
He reminded readers that Game of Thrones directors
David Benioff and D.B. Weiss are actively

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:19.000
developing their own Star Wars saga, about
which very little is currently known.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:23.500
Last we heard, Rian Johnson was still tinkering
away with development on his own film trilogy.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:27.860
The company is also developing at least one
big-budget TV show for its upcoming streaming

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:28.360
service.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:32.580
But this content, Iger said, will be served
up a little more judiciously, with the company

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.620
being, quote, "a little bit more careful about
volume and timing."

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.260
The suggestion that Disney should throttle
down on the Star Wars movies isn't a particularly

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.020
new idea.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:46.040
Fans and commentators have been talking about
the potential consequences of Star Wars oversaturation

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:51.120
for a while, with the once-unthinkable term
"Star Wars fatigue" becoming a frequent talking

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:51.620
point.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:56.680
The franchise took a major hit with the massively
underperforming Solo: A Star Wars Story, which

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:00.520
essentially bombed at the box office after
a notoriously troubled production.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.380
Following that high-profile disappointment,
a whole lot of people got on board with the

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:09.100
idea that Disney's strategy for Star Wars
was actually killing interest in the once-venerable

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:09.600
franchise.

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:13.939
The common wisdom was that Lucasfilm was trying
too hard to mimic the release calendar of

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:16.960
Marvel Studios, and it's backfiring in a big
way.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:21.700
While there are fans and viewers who are perfectly
happy with the current state of Star Wars,

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:25.470
there's a vocal segment of the fanbase that's
all-but-begging for Disney to get its act

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:29.900
together as the series wraps up the Skywalker
Saga and moves on to stories beyond.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:33.580
Iger's comments seem to suggest that those
fans are finally being heard.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.340
Though it's refreshing to hear Iger straight-up
admitting Disney dropped the ball, is it possible

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:39.920
that the damage is already done?

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:41.240
That's hard to predict.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.260
Already, some feel that Disney has bigger
problems with its Star Wars approach than

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:46.820
the high volume of releases.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:51.640
After the interview, THR writer Graeme McMillan
questioned whether "volume and timing are

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:55.810
really to blame," and suggested that perhaps
the audience has a problem with these specific

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:56.980
Star Wars movies.

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:00.880
In other words, a lot of people might just
dislike the stories that Disney's telling

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:04.200
— and that's a harder problem to fix than
just stretching out a release schedule.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:08.140
Still, it seems that the people in charge
at Disney are doing some serious reflection

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.820
about how they might have mangled their very
expensive space fantasy series.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.320
A less-dense release schedule is a good start.

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:19.320
As to whether or not that will be enough to
bring the magic back to Star Wars, that remains

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:19.759
to be seen.

